#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfbe689c-ae04-5405-9a38-d80f10662a0b" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Horizontal Hyperbola in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b50fd87-9a94-519a-b2df-b7c7fbd644a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291363} {:answer/id #uuid "33bd661e-3a94-5a8d-9ad0-ef287e924386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291364} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ad4a90-4398-5697-b748-eb04f4a456fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291365} {:answer/id #uuid "0119602e-ae05-5631-a3c6-326cf98ca0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291366} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cf1c64-d8e0-50ff-83cd-f5b85bd3fdcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0119602e-ae05-5631-a3c6-326cf98ca0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291366} :answer-field/id #uuid "370cd7ad-7240-594d-ad13-7fd0f8b42fe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2af82f3-9d93-5885-b2ca-b940ebfe011c" :question/math-academy-id "q-103912" :question/problem "Write the equation of the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $18$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{18}{18} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ec37c5e-8a40-58a7-8af3-4457cd5da43f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1c71444-dd4a-57b1-96b8-200c7235140d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291370} {:answer/id #uuid "398dfc9d-8215-548e-8963-d32de0b78d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291371} {:answer/id #uuid "36a888a2-9999-59ec-9cd8-229ed3030d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291372} {:answer/id #uuid "afbd5857-1419-532c-abc1-5c27e3bea386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291373} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8024b9-98a7-5742-8975-44f2db7f5d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c71444-dd4a-57b1-96b8-200c7235140d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291370} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d960efe-ceb6-58e1-832b-78da060375a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e51393b-cf1d-5d4b-917b-3f067d1f544f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120347" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28822b93-4c9d-5cc5-aab7-f110c323de0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291377} {:answer/id #uuid "c16212fb-8f3a-52ce-83aa-03b289b43dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378} {:answer/id #uuid "420d6e97-d13a-5227-b04d-1808fae2deae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291379} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b19b9e-fb87-54ad-a6d2-fce696977322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291380} {:answer/id #uuid "748616c3-4625-57a9-b33a-1e6c83d856f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b19b9e-fb87-54ad-a6d2-fce696977322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291380} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc192ef-45f1-53c0-98e5-8cc69d753029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "490c0980-5ac4-561f-af02-cd8ffe3a3650" :question/math-academy-id "q-122298" :question/problem "In box $A,$ there are $7$ red marbles and $3$ green marbles. In box $B,$ there are $2$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. If we randomly select one of the boxes and then we randomly select one marble from that box, what is the probability that it’s a green marble?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that box $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that box $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ boxes, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $A$ and $8$  green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(G|{A}^{′})=\\frac{8}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{8}{10})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d877fa7e-da62-5607-8a68-7a6113b8b1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291384} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb598f4-aef5-5831-9035-65cb7926c515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291385} {:answer/id #uuid "743f3559-fd45-50aa-b268-6428f816ad57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291386} {:answer/id #uuid "d2294ad2-4fda-5263-9605-89cc1cb44fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{{9}^{x}}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1cc4b6-9bdc-537d-b468-0e8a81608623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb598f4-aef5-5831-9035-65cb7926c515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291385} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f6d1ae-18fd-535a-9588-2fec5296aec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ac5a63d-ba5d-5b91-87a0-a208698bb5b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2281" :question/problem "$\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc6da4f-977d-5a2e-8bf9-f1f8f971be13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291391} {:answer/id #uuid "2244df98-3e18-5a80-80fb-b36bc6f00242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291392} {:answer/id #uuid "eb42405b-9467-54a1-abcc-bf4ffc8f85ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291393} {:answer/id #uuid "404073ca-72fb-5104-992d-5dba92e853c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291394} {:answer/id #uuid "87caf5f7-1192-5784-9e83-bb02335c1516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2244df98-3e18-5a80-80fb-b36bc6f00242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291392} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2273bf-a71f-5940-95ea-711271f7116a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e71680c0-b9f9-54f6-9973-5f9558436a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-28341" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $16x+4y=12?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $16x+4y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x+4y & =12 \\\\ 4y & =-16x+12 \\\\ y & =-4x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9febd2a0-a762-5056-9012-cfc02c47f0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{5}{3}\\tan \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082311} {:answer/id #uuid "b6401d38-eddc-5c0b-b937-14dad650e0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{5}{3}\\cot \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082312} {:answer/id #uuid "c81e3e74-d623-52ac-bac9-f2edf238501c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{3}{5}\\tan \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082313} {:answer/id #uuid "de181b10-09cd-5b2a-9334-bc4e69fd7bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cot \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082314} {:answer/id #uuid "5401f442-4323-578b-a969-4bd160377cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{5}{3}\\tan \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5401f442-4323-578b-a969-4bd160377cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{5}{3}\\tan \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082315} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf28272-402e-55be-8b1d-96bc35cfe76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e23a09c-558b-5f22-8055-2a530503094c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6731" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d38fdfd-fe89-587f-bde9-cafd1e17f69b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291398} {:answer/id #uuid "b86b3061-41b3-5095-9d00-e339d320775d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291399} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0ef3c9-9503-5d85-bf7e-bbc0d320a329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291400} {:answer/id #uuid "590f7a82-534e-5881-a2a4-b60a0c9c5df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291401} {:answer/id #uuid "807bbff5-24cd-5cf6-a308-25aab9d0d846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0ef3c9-9503-5d85-bf7e-bbc0d320a329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291400} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c3a0ae-7715-5855-a1ff-9efcb9e5816c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e4d9326-2ae7-55cb-8a17-5cc3ba49aafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73084" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-2,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e9c6ad-9a69-58b4-8c48-c287e48acefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291405} {:answer/id #uuid "212151ed-6e07-5059-8cb9-3223491b3e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291406} {:answer/id #uuid "b28ced1c-ba2f-556c-8e94-a11ac048a826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291407} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9411cd-30b4-508b-a18d-15dba3195615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291408} {:answer/id #uuid "5402e8d4-9bd8-572c-80f7-13ef8ac7a5ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe9411cd-30b4-508b-a18d-15dba3195615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291408} :answer-field/id #uuid "486ac45d-503f-5e45-9911-203081d171d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e6bb51b-2f06-5367-b05a-e25bda6eecb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-74771" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a520369b-49fd-51b0-9754-de9d41a44a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291412} {:answer/id #uuid "5989debf-8bdb-535b-80c6-4848118fa786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291413} {:answer/id #uuid "a232c682-ce3f-5a5f-bc5d-f2ecc22db422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291414} {:answer/id #uuid "23b4cc7f-373c-5676-9712-77eca3bb13ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291415} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf57d5e-467b-56f1-bb1d-e78f686e3d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5989debf-8bdb-535b-80c6-4848118fa786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291413} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf78a22-36ed-512a-a6a8-c5329c6ca6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4eaae104-8d2e-5865-b201-2de2f96bdaac" :question/math-academy-id "q-81576" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are local minimums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nAdding $5$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5\\ge 1+5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at the local minimum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171968fc-c276-590d-a500-80478856f419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5{a}^{4}+1)(5{a}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291419} {:answer/id #uuid "c8680d22-29fe-5afa-9480-6389382ab266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291420} {:answer/id #uuid "78eb12da-f03f-575d-b0bf-9626c6094b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291421} {:answer/id #uuid "d759732d-52a0-57f9-9327-a7aac712055d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25({a}^{4}+\\text{i})({a}^{4}-\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291422} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac9f49d-b86d-5612-88da-c4a925ff8389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(5{a}^{2}-\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78eb12da-f03f-575d-b0bf-9626c6094b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291421} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c7de78e-3a06-5076-bd54-20425ccb2318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e91ac095-0027-50ac-b8fd-5be518c91581" :question/math-academy-id "q-87327" :question/problem "Factor $25{a}^{8}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n25{a}^{8}+1={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{a}^{8}+1 & =25{a}^{8}-(-1) \\\\  & =25{a}^{8}-({\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}-{(\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed993ab-3334-5ccb-a6d1-de46de32e2b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082779} {:answer/id #uuid "c1417fa0-3a83-59a1-a948-68abe3b13f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082780} {:answer/id #uuid "d7515743-1344-5824-b3a8-4616c8d7f656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082781} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec055dc-e2f9-5751-bc1a-216a83a3d1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082782} {:answer/id #uuid "775fffd3-602e-5674-becb-000f3809408a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775fffd3-602e-5674-becb-000f3809408a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082783} :answer-field/id #uuid "80746c4b-d140-5b2c-93e5-fb42d3b92ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f95addf-2f10-5fef-bf09-d016f4ff31e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-98719" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{{S}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(S-1,\\,S+1,\\,(S-1)(S+1))=(S-1)(S+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $S=1$ and $S=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1} & =\\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ (S-1)(S+1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}) & =(S-1)(S+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ \\frac{(S-1)(S+1)}{S+1}+\\frac{2(S-1)(S+1)}{S-1} & =\\frac{(S-1)(S+1)}{(S-1)(S+1)} \\\\ \\frac{(S-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(S+1)}}{\\cancel{S+1}}+\\frac{2(S+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(S-1)}}{\\cancel{S-1}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(S-1)(S+1)}}{\\cancel{(S-1)(S+1)}} \\\\ S-1+2(S+1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $S,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S-1+2(S+1) & =1 \\\\ S-1+2S+2 & =1 \\\\ 3S & =0 \\\\ S & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\ne 1$ and $0\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $S=0$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $S=0.$"}]}
